## Authoritative Sources &amp; Evidence Base
*What the research actually says — and how it should change how you prompt.*
### 🔵 Foundational Papers
- **Brown et al. (2020).** &quot;Language Models are Few-Shot Learners.&quot; *NeurIPS.* [arXiv:2005.14165](arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165)
	Established that scaling LLMs enables few-shot learning through prompting alone. The paper that made prompting a programming interface, not a workaround.
- **Wei et al. (2022).** &quot;Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models.&quot; *NeurIPS.* [arXiv:2201.11903](arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903)
	Step-by-step reasoning improves performance on complex tasks by 10–30%. A 540B model with just 8 CoT exemplars reached SOTA on GSM8K math benchmarks. The empirical foundation for CoT.
- **Yao et al. (2023).** &quot;Tree of Thoughts: Deliberate Problem Solving with Large Language Models.&quot; *NeurIPS.* [arXiv:2305.10601](arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601)
	Generalizes CoT with multi-path exploration and backtracking. GPT-4 + CoT solved 4% of Game of 24; ToT solved 74%. Use for non-linear, high-search-space problems where first-path reasoning fails.
- **Yao et al. (2023).** &quot;ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models.&quot; *ICLR.*
	Combined reasoning traces with tool use in a single loop. The conceptual foundation for agentic orchestration patterns in this VSM system.





### 🟡 Context, Format &amp; Length Research
&lt;callout icon=&quot;⚠️&quot; color=&quot;yellow_bg&quot;&gt;
	These three papers directly govern how prompts in this system are structured. They override intuition. Read before designing any Tier 3+ prompt.
&lt;/callout&gt;
- **Tam et al. (2024).** &quot;Let Me Speak Freely? A Study on the Impact of Format Restrictions on LLM Performance.&quot; *arXiv:2408.02442*
	Rigid output format constraints degrade reasoning accuracy by **up to 64 percentage points.** Rule: think first, format second. Never front-load structural constraints before reasoning steps.
- **Levy, Jacoby &amp; Goldberg (2024).** &quot;Same Task, More Tokens: The Impact of Input Length on LLM Reasoning Performance.&quot; *arXiv:2402.14848*
	Accuracy dropped from 92% to 68% when irrelevant context surrounded the same task. Rule: trim noise ruthlessly. Irrelevance is the enemy, not length.
- **Liu, Wang &amp; Willard (2025).** &quot;Effects of Prompt Length and Complexity on LLM Performance for Domain-Specific Tasks.&quot; *arXiv:2502.14255*
	Longer prompts with **relevant** detail improved performance across all nine tested tasks. Confirms: density of relevant context helps; padding destroys.


### 🔴 Critical / Revisionist Research
- **Meincke, Mollick, Mollick &amp; Shapiro (2025).** &quot;The Decreasing Value of Chain of Thought in Prompting.&quot; *Wharton GAIL / SSRN:5285532.* [Full report](gail.wharton.upenn.edu/research-and-insights/tech-report-chain-of-thought/)
	On frontier reasoning models, explicit CoT yields only marginal gains at 20–80% additional time cost. Non-reasoning models show modest improvement but higher variance. Rule: **tier technique selection by model type.** CoT is no longer universally beneficial. Default to native model reasoning; add explicit CoT only when output is demonstrably inconsistent.


### 🟢 Surveys
- **Sahoo et al. (2024).** &quot;A Systematic Survey of Prompt Engineering in Large Language Models: Techniques and Applications.&quot; *arXiv:2402.07927* [Link](arxiv.org/abs/2402.07927)
	Most comprehensive academic taxonomy. Covers zero-shot, few-shot, CoT, instruction tuning, soft prompts, and multimodal prompting. Use as the canonical reference when selecting or justifying a technique.
- **ScienceDirect / Patterns (2025).** &quot;Unleashing the Potential of Prompt Engineering for Large Language Models.&quot; [Link](www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666389925001084)
	Covers self-consistency, generated knowledge prompting, VLM techniques, and adversarial security (jailbreak defense). Relevant when activating Guardian mode in Safe Prompt Validation workflows.


### ⚙️ Practitioner References
- [**DAIR.AI**](http://DAIR.AI)** Prompt Engineering Guide** — [promptingguide.ai](promptingguide.ai) / [Papers index](www.promptingguide.ai/papers)
	Most actively maintained practitioner reference. Tracks latest techniques, model-specific guides, and new research. Use as a living index for technique lookup before building.
- **thunlp/PromptPapers** — [GitHub](github.com/thunlp/PromptPapers)
	Curated must-read paper list on prompt-based tuning, maintained by NLP researchers. Use to trace the intellectual lineage of any technique before citing it in a prompt rationale.


### 🏠 Internal Workspace References
- &lt;mention-page url=&quot;app.notion.com/p/a2eadb6090c8422d8bd860396327e86d&quot;/&gt; — Michael&#039;s handbook page. COSTAR framework, failure mode table, zero/few-shot, CoT. Practitioner SSoT for this workspace.
- &lt;mention-page url=&quot;app.notion.com/p/0176976735d3423da73dcadadcbc2890&quot;/&gt; — Full academic bibliography for the AI Products handbook. Includes Vaswani (2017), Bubeck (2023), full ReAct citation, and the Gao (2023) RAG survey.

